You can do this by using a loop to keep asking the user for input until they enter a valid number. Here's an example of how you could implement this:
public int displayOptions(String[] options) {
    Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    int choice = 0;
    boolean valid = false;

    while (!valid) {
        System.out.println("What would you like to do?");
        for (int i = 0; i < options.length; i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + options[i]);
        }

        System.out.print("Enter your choice: ");
        String input = scanner.nextLine();

        try {
            choice = Integer.parseInt(input);
            if (choice >= 1 && choice <= options.length) {
                valid = true;
            } else {
                System.out.println("Invalid choice. Please enter a number between 1 and " + options.length + ".");
            }
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a number.");
        }
    }

    return choice;
}
In this method, we first print out the options to the user. Then we ask them to enter their choice. We use a try-catch block to handle any NumberFormatException that might occur if the user enters non-numeric input. We also check if the input is within the valid range of options. If it is, we set valid to true and exit the loop. If not, we print an error message and ask again.
Note that this method uses a Scanner to read input from the user. If you're using a different input method, you'll need to adjust the code accordingly.
